---
title: Automatic differentiation over frames
summary: How a straight-line program is differentiated by composing each step's derivative maps, forward with frames of tangents, in reverse with frames of cotangents, and forward-over-reverse for Hessian products.
---

<script>
	import Statement from '$lib/components/Statement.svelte';
	import Proof from '$lib/components/Proof.svelte';
	import * as forwardOverReverse from './statements/forward-over-reverse.md';
</script>

Automatic differentiation computes the derivatives of a program exactly, up to rounding, from the
derivatives of the steps it is made of. This note states the three ways of composing them that a
library needs (pushing tangents forward, pulling cotangents back, and differentiating the pull-back
once more for second order), each over a *frame* of several directions at once.

## Programs

A *straight-line program* has slots $s_0, s_1, \dots$, each holding a vector, and runs a sequence of
steps

$$
	s_{o_k} \leftarrow F_k\bigl(s_{i_{k,1}}, \dots, s_{i_{k,m_k}}\bigr), @tag(step)
$$

each writing one slot from slots written earlier. Some slots are *leaves*, supplied from outside:
the variables $x$. Some are *targets*, the values to differentiate, $f(x)$. Each step's function is
smooth, with partial Jacobians $\partial_j F_k$, one per input, of shape (output dimension) by
(dimension of input $j$). When an input is not differentiated, its partial Jacobian is simply never
used.

A *frame* is a matrix whose $k$ columns are directions: a tangent frame at a slot of dimension $d$
is a $d \times k$ matrix $\dot S$, a cotangent frame a $d \times k$ matrix $\alpha$ whose columns
are linear functionals, written as columns.

## Forward: pushing tangent frames

Seed each leaf with a tangent frame $\dot X$ (missing means zero), and run the steps in order, each
pushing its inputs' frames forward by its *Jacobian–vector product*:

$$
	\dot S_{o_k} = \sum_j \partial_j F_k \; \dot S_{i_{k,j}} . @tag(jvp)
$$

By the chain rule, the frame reaching a target is $\sum_l \partial f / \partial x_l \cdot \dot X_l$:
one pass with a frame of $k$ columns gives $k$ directional derivatives, and seeding the columns of
an identity gives the whole Jacobian.

## Reverse: pulling cotangent frames

Seed each target with a cotangent frame $\alpha_f$, every other slot with zero, and run the steps
backwards, each adding its *vector–Jacobian product*, the pull-back of its output's cotangent, into
each input:

$$
	\alpha_{i_{k,j}} \mathrel{+}= \partial_j F_k\transpose \, \alpha_{o_k} . @tag(vjp)
$$

The accumulation, which sums the contributions of every step that reads a slot, is the chain rule
for a slot with several consumers. When the steps are done, each leaf holds
$\alpha_{x_l} = \sum_f (\partial f / \partial x_l)\transpose \alpha_f$. For a scalar target seeded
with $\alpha_f = 1$, that is the gradient $\nabla_{x_l} f$, whatever the number of leaves.

Each column of a frame costs a small multiple of one evaluation of the program, in either
direction. A Jacobian with few rows is cheaper in reverse, one with few columns forward.

## Forward-over-reverse: Hessian products

For second derivatives, differentiate the reverse pass itself along a path $x + t\dot x$. Run the
forward pass of [](eq:jvp) with velocities $\dot X$ seeded at the leaves, so that every slot holds
its value $s$ and its velocity $\dot S$, then run the reverse pass carrying two cotangents per slot:
$\alpha_s$, as in [](eq:vjp), and $\beta_s$, its derivative along the path. One column of
$\dot X$ is one path; a frame of $k$ columns runs $k$ paths at once, and then $\beta_s$ is a frame
of $k$ columns while $\alpha_s$ is the same for all of them. (A cotangent frame of several columns
together with a velocity frame of several columns would make $\beta$ a three-way array; the passes
here take one cotangent column.)

Differentiating [](eq:vjp) along the path by the product rule gives

$$
	\beta_{i_{k,j}} \mathrel{+}= \operatorname{hvp}_{k,j}(\alpha_{o_k}) + \partial_j F_k\transpose \, \beta_{o_k},
	\qquad
	\operatorname{hvp}_{k,j}(\alpha) \defeq \frac{\mathrm d}{\mathrm dt}\Big|_{t=0} \partial_j F_k(s + t\dot S)\transpose \alpha
	= \sum_l \bigl(\partial_l \partial_j F_k\,[\dot S_{i_{k,l}}]\bigr)\transpose \alpha . @tag(leibniz)
$$

The first term is the step's own second-order map, which depends on the velocities of its inputs;
the second is the step's ordinary pull-back, applied to $\beta$. A library writes the first for
each step and the program applies the rule. Two consequences are useful when writing
$\operatorname{hvp}$ for a step. If a step is linear, its partial Jacobians are constant and its
$\operatorname{hvp}$ is zero. If the pull-back into input $j$ does not involve input $l$, then the
velocity of input $l$ contributes nothing to $\operatorname{hvp}_{k,j}$.

<Statement {...forwardOverReverse} />

<Proof>

The cotangents $\alpha_s$ are functions of the leaves: the reverse recurrence holds as an identity
in $x$, with every $\partial_j F_k$ evaluated at the slot values the program computes from $x$.
Differentiate that identity along $x + t\dot x$ for one column $\dot x$ of $\dot X$. By the
product rule, $\beta_s \defeq \frac{\mathrm d}{\mathrm dt}\alpha_s(x + t\dot x)\big|_{t=0}$
satisfies [](eq:leibniz), because the slot values move with the velocities the forward pass
computed, and the seeds $\alpha_f = 1$ and $\beta_f = 0$ are the value and the derivative of a
constant. At a leaf, $\alpha_{x_i}(x) = \nabla_{x_i} f(x)$ identically in $x$, so its derivative
along the path is $\sum_l \nabla_{x_i} \nabla_{x_l} f(x)\,\dot x_l$.

</Proof>

With all leaves stacked into one vector, $\beta_x = \nabla^2 f(x)\,\dot X$: a frame of $k$
velocities gives $k$ columns of the Hessian for about $k$ times the cost of a gradient, and the
identity frame gives all of it.
